<Docs
  title="Durability"
  description="What happens on a crash, a merge, a damaged file, or two people writing at once."
  summary="The repository is the database, which puts the whole weight of the design on the write path."
>
  <h2>Writes are atomic</h2>

  <p>
    Every file cairn writes — items, the roadmap, the configuration, exports —
    goes to a temporary file beside its target, is flushed to the device, and is
    renamed over it. Rename is atomic within a filesystem everywhere cairn runs,
    so an interruption leaves either the old contents or the new ones. Nothing
    is truncated in place.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a test that kills the program mid-write forty times in a row and
    checks the item is still whole each time.
  </p>

  <h2>Line endings survive</h2>

  <p>
    An item file is read with either line ending and written back with the one
    it arrived with. Without that, a checkout configured with
    <code>core.autocrlf</code> would have every write reformat the whole file,
    and a backlog shared across platforms would churn continuously.
  </p>

  <p>
    New items are written with line feeds, and <code>cairn init</code> installs
    a <code>.gitattributes</code> pinning the item directory to
    <code>eol=lf</code>, so a repository has one answer regardless of how each
    contributor's client is configured.
  </p>

  <h2>One damaged file does not stop everything</h2>

  <p>
    Read-only commands — <code>list</code>, <code>next</code>,
    <code>board</code>, <code>search</code>, <code>roadmap</code> — report a
    file they cannot parse and carry on with the rest. A backlog should not
    become unreadable because one file is mid-edit.
  </p>

  <p>
    Everything else refuses. <code>check</code>, <code>render</code>,
    <code>export</code> and every command that writes stop with an error,
    because acting on a partial view is how data is lost: rendering a roadmap
    that silently omits an item, or allocating an identifier that a broken file
    already holds.
  </p>

  <h2>Branches merge without a fight</h2>

  <p>
    Two branches that both add an item conflict in the generated roadmap and
    collide on an identifier, on the first merge. Neither is really a conflict —
    both files are derived — so the answer is to derive them again.
  </p>

  <pre><code>cairn init --git</code></pre>

  <p>
    That registers a merge driver for the roadmap and a <code>post-merge</code>
    hook that renumbers collisions and re-renders. It is safe on an existing
    project, and doing it twice changes nothing.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ordering constrains the design and is worth knowing. Git resolves paths
    in index order and <code>ROADMAP.md</code> sorts before
    <code>cairn/items/</code>, so when the driver runs the items it would render
    from are still unmerged. It therefore keeps your side and the hook replaces
    it once the items settle. A merge performed by a forge runs no hook at all,
    which is why <code>cairn check --render</code> belongs in continuous
    integration regardless.
  </p>

  <h2>Identifiers</h2>

  <p>
    Item identifiers are small integers because people have to type them. That
    has a cost worth stating plainly: allocating the next one means knowing
    every one in use, which requires coordination that branches do not provide.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>cairn check</code> detects a collision and names both files.
    <code>cairn renumber</code> repairs it — the older item keeps the contested
    number and the one that arrived later moves, so the repair matches what
    happened. Renumbering is never automatic outside the merge hook: it rewrites
    files, and that should happen because you asked.
  </p>

  <h2>The format will not move under you</h2>

  <p>
    A project records the format version it uses. A minor release may add
    optional keys and nothing else; removing a key or changing its meaning needs
    a new version and a migration; a reader preserves keys it does not
    recognise, so an older cairn cannot silently strip what a newer one wrote.
  </p>

  <p>
    A project written in a format this build does not know is refused with both
    numbers named, rather than read on a best-effort basis. Misreading data is
    worse than declining to read it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is tested</h2>

  <p>
    The frontmatter parser handles input typed by people, generated by models
    and carried in from other trackers, so it is property-tested rather than
    only exercised by examples: what cairn writes it reads back unchanged,
    rendering twice is byte-identical, line endings survive, and arbitrary bytes
    may be rejected but never cause a crash.
  </p>

  <p>
    A corpus of item files, including ones cairn would never write, must keep
    parsing to the same values forever. And a soak test drives long random
    sequences of ordinary operations, checking after every step that the backlog
    still holds together — it found three real bugs on its first afternoon.
  </p>
</Docs>
